module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.readback_expectation_rendered_over_a_refused_read_under_the_wrong_principal

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, RungRead, FoundOnRung, BelowCeiling }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

data readback_expectation_rendered_over_a_refused_read_under_the_wrong_principal: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "readback_expectation_rendered_over_a_refused_read_under_the_wrong_principal" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "a candidate expected_surface_identity that cannot be written from any observation arm except a rendered bundle, so minting from a refused roster read cannot be spelled",
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: the live-deploy candidate mints its expected served-surface identity by rendering the roadmap site surface over an event-log read that the LAUNCHING PRINCIPAL could not open, and the cutover gate then compares that fabricated expectation against the live service's render of the SAME log under a principal that CAN open it. HARM: readiness fails deterministically (ProcessSurfaceMismatch) on a deploy where every leg applied and the services are healthy, and the failure is not fixable by redeploying because both sides are deterministic. It costs a full apply cycle and an operator approval to learn a fact the launcher could have named in milliseconds.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACT: one semantic question (what roster should the admitted candidate serve) is rendered by two PRINCIPALS over two OWNERSHIPS of the same carrier. The live service runs as the instance principal (srv1: briansrls), opens the snapshot, reads every node's events, and renders the real roster. The candidate (gunbc.live_deploy.candidate observe_candidate_release) runs in the CI job as the runner principal (srv1: ghrunner) but rendered through the INSTANCE carrier layout (roadmap_event_carrier_layout_for_instance: repo_root /opt/gunbc/gunbc, worktree /opt/gunbc/roadmap-events, both briansrls-owned). The carrier read is NOT read-only (git worktree add in repo_root, fetch, reset --hard), so under a foreign principal EVERY git call refuses with 'detected dubious ownership' and the runner cannot write the instance .git. This is the PRINCIPAL axis of gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::readback_expectation_rederived_under_a_different_binding (which is the BINDING axis: two producers of one digest). Same family, different axis.",

    "RECEIPT (2026-11-21, bright-ibex-807, run 44966880833, job 137040837008, main 7711690f, fleet-converge mode=dashboard_deploy on srv1): every leg applied; the cutover gate then refused 'ServiceNotReady unit=gunbc-roadmap.service ... ProcessSurfaceMismatch expected=932452bbee40480a observed=bca26f0704e77c8c'; receipt apply_outcome=not_converged while the live service, the door and fabric-storage were all running 7711690f and serving. Read-only reproduction: sudo -u ghrunner -H git -C /opt/gunbc/roadmap-events rev-parse HEAD -> 'fatal: detected dubious ownership in repository at /opt/gunbc/roadmap-events'. ghrunner's ~/.gitconfig safe.directory listed only /srv/gunbc-deploy/gunbc. In the deploy log gunbc.WitnessBin.Run finished in 2ms right after Filesystem.List /opt/gunbc/roadmap-events — the signature of a roster read that refused immediately. No event-log writes occurred in the window, so it was not a race.",

    "REPAIR (this PR): gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_site_surface_observe gained the typed observation roadmap_site_surface_bundle_observation_of_layout (any layout) and TWO principal-chosen wrappers — roadmap_site_surface_bundle_observation (the SRV1 INSTANCE layout, for callers running as the instance principal) and roadmap_site_surface_bundle_observation_for_launcher (the CI-RUNNER layout, for callers running in the fleet-converge deploy job as ghrunner) — both folding the plan read EXACTLY ONCE and naming the refusal (RoadmapSiteSurfaceBundleReadRefused) instead of rendering a bundle over it. ROOT GENERALIZATION (gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization): every site that MINTS AN EXPECTATION from the surface flows through that one fold, and each is bound to the layout its OWN principal can open (chain re-derivation, gunbc.design_argument::earliest_unjustified_boundary). (1) gunbc.live_deploy.candidate gained CandidateUnobservableCause::CandidateRosterReadRefused and the pure fold candidate_observation_from_bundle_observation, which REFUSES the candidate — an unobservable candidate never enters the mutation path and a refused roster never supplies an expected identity; it observes via the LAUNCHER observation. (2) The shared readiness gate observe_service_readiness_from_healthz now takes the observation itself and returns Refused{ServiceSurfaceUnobserved} on a refused read. CRITICAL, and the first review's blocking finding: the cutover readiness gate and the launch receipt CI step run in the SAME deploy job as the candidate (as ghrunner, in the job's checkout), so they read via the LAUNCHER observation too — NOT the instance observation. Had readiness kept the instance layout it would only have renamed the deterministic failure from ProcessSurfaceMismatch to a late ServiceSurfaceUnobserved; the deploy-route control pins that the layout they build has repo_root = git.Inspect.Toplevel() (the runner checkout), not the briansrls-owned /opt/gunbc. (3) roadmap_belt_tick_cli mints expected_surface via the INSTANCE observation because the belt tick is spawned under the belt service's OWN principal/slice/spawn root on the instance tree — a different principal, so a different (correct) layout; a refused read there refuses to ReleaseBindingUnobserved. (4) roadmap_site_healthz_validate.roadmap_site_healthz_surface_is_current_from_observed_body (a comparison, no production caller today) observes the expected side and answers NOT-current on a refusal. SURVIVORS, each answered: materialize_live_roadmap_site_surface_bundle is now the SERVE-SIDE renderer only — the process's own /healthz bytes (roadmap_static_site roadmap_site_healthz_body / _for_release) and the well-formedness check roadmap_site_healthz_body_parses — which render the OBSERVED side under the authority that serves it, not an expectation compared across a boundary; the trigger that moves any of them onto the observation is the day one mints an identity another process compares against. The read itself (option a, chosen over option b): candidate/readiness/launch render over roadmap_event_carrier_ci_layout — repo_root = the runner's OWN checkout, remote origin, branch roadmap-events — so they read the same authority the service renders from (the service's roadmap_event_carrier_sync does fetch <remote> <branch>; reset --hard FETCH_HEAD on every read, so the remote's tip is THE shared authority and a divergent local head is discarded, never rendered), under the principal that owns it. The carrier read is NOT read-only (worktree add / fetch / reset --hard), so the layout creates the carrier WORKTREE in a fresh runner-owned mktemp -d scratch OUTSIDE the deploy checkout (a unique scratch per read, never a fixed shared /tmp path — extdeps.git.inspect::Toplevel records a review against the latter): the read runs in repo_root and writes objects, worktree registrations and temporary trees into the runner's .git — it is NOT a read-only ref peek — but no tracked path of the checkout is touched, so git status and the deploy cleanliness check (which read tracked content) stay clean. Option (b), reading as the instance principal through an admitted write grant, was rejected: the carrier read WRITES, so the read-only -c safe.directory pattern cannot authorize it and a foreign-principal write grant is not admissible. No host edit, no safe.directory write, no chown.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: a candidate-side render of a host-owned, read-not-only authority whose expected identity can differ from the live service's for a reason that is purely WHO is reading. The tell is a launcher that renders through the instance layout while running as a different user than the service, and a surface renderer that returns a bundle (with a tagged 'subject-refused' identity) rather than a refusal when the underlying projection is RoadmapAuthorityProjectionRefused. Read the deploy log for a WitnessBin.Run that finishes in single-digit milliseconds after a Filesystem.List of the event-log directory.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable — the mismatch was loud (typed ProcessSurfaceMismatch at the cutover gate), not silent wrongness, but it was far too late (~55 min, after apply, after approval). CEILING: structurally guaranteed once the only producer of a candidate's expected surface identity is the live observation over a layout the launching principal can read, and a refused roster read has no path to mint an identity at all. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a type that makes a candidate expected_surface_identity unwritable from any observation arm except a rendered bundle, so minting from a refused read cannot be spelled.",

    "CONSUMPTION: this per-class record joins the derived gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster recurring_failure_mode_roster. HERMETIC controls (test.claim.live_deploy.candidate_surface_roster_refusal_witness, run BY HAND on the mocked claim lane where git/mktemp are host mocks; NOT executed by CI, because CI runs no witness): a_refused_roster_read_refuses_the_candidate (red before the change: the fold had no refusal arm and always returned CandidateObserved), the_refusal_cause_names_the_refused_roster_and_the_absent_identity, the_ci_side_expectation_equals_the_service_side_render_over_the_same_event_set (a fold-consistency tripwire, not a two-read proof: the candidate copies the bundle_identity and dispatch digest of the ONE bundle the service renders from the same authority, so it never recomputes a divergent identity), an_unanswered_read_is_a_refusal_and_never_a_renderable_surface, the_readiness_gate_refuses_a_refused_roster_read_and_never_renders_a_verdict (red before the change: the gate took a bare bundle and compared a served body against one minted over a refused read; now it returns Refused{ServiceSurfaceUnobserved}). WET controls (test.manual.candidate_launcher_read_real_execution — these perform REAL git and filesystem effects, are NOT executed by CI, and are run by hand on the wet lane per that module's header): launcher_control_repeats_and_cleans_up runs the REAL launcher read (roadmap_events_read_all under roadmap_event_carrier_ci_layout) TWICE against ONE checkout with origin unchanged, asserting both render the same non-empty committed-fixture roster, the snapshot CLOSES on each read, the runner worktree registry is empty again, nothing materializes inside the checkout, and the named branch is never attached — RED when the layout or lifecycle is reverted, where a branch-attached anchor leaves registry=1 and a worktree inside the checkout; launcher_control_branch_attached_lifecycle_collides drives the OLD branch-attached lifecycle twice and shows the SECOND read refusing (refusing to fetch into branch ... checked out) with a leaked registration — the collision the ScopedDetached read removes (red before the change); launcher_control_two_readers_agree is the genuine two-read positive control: two independent launcher reads of one origin render the same roster; launcher_control_ref_assertion_discriminates proves the branch-ref assertion is a real judgement rather than suppressed stdout, reporting the named branch ABSENT (show-ref exit status 1) on the real scoped read and PRESENT (exit status 0) after the prohibited branch:branch fetch operand; launcher_control_failed_add_is_cleaned_up plants a post-checkout hook that fails the worktree add AFTER it registered, asserting the read refuses at the snapshot step, the close reports a real Closed (it verified registration and directory are gone, never SnapshotNotOpened for state that existed), and the runner holds no registration; launcher_control_real_reader_locked_moved_add_is_caught drives the REAL scoped reader with a post-checkout hook that, AFTER git has registered the worktree, LOCKS it, MOVES the checked-out tree to a known path, SYMLINKS the vacated path to it, and fails the add, so the registration survives at the canonical path while the directory is reachable only through a symlink; it asserts the read refuses at the snapshot step, the runner still holds the registration, and the close does NOT claim Closed — the reader established the canonical identity BEFORE the add (before the hook sabotaged the path), so a correct close compares against the true registration and reports a leak — RED on the pre-fix verifier, which canonicalized AFTER the sabotage, resolved the symlink to the moved tree, compared the registration to the wrong string, saw nothing, and minted a false SnapshotClosed (the hook writes a marker only if BOTH its `mv` and `ln` succeeded, so the control proves the sabotage ran rather than trusting `|| true`); `launcher_control_pre_add_identity_failure_reports_surviving_scratch` injects a failing `realpath_program` that plants an undeletable subtree in the mktemp scratch and exits non-zero, so the reader enters the pre-add arm, its `rm` FAILS, and the close MUST report the surviving directory as a directory leak — RED on the pre-fix unchecked-`rm` arm that always claimed SnapshotNotOpened; THE IDENTITY IS BOUND TO THE ADD, NOT RESOLVED AT CLOSE: the scoped read establishes the CANONICAL identity with realpath of the scratch WHILE it still exists and BEFORE `git worktree add`, registers the worktree AT THAT CANONICAL PATH, and carries it (with the original mktemp allocation) through every arm into the close, which verifies against the carried values and never re-resolves realpath; a realpath failure is the pre-add refusal route: NO worktree was registered, but the scratch mktemp just created is OURS, so the arm removes it and then OBSERVES its presence — `SnapshotNotOpened` is claimed ONLY when absence is established, and a surviving or unobservable scratch is a typed directory leak (`CloseVerifyDirectoryPersists` / `CloseVerifyDirectoryPresenceUnobservable`), never a silent `SnapshotNotOpened` (the pre-fix arm did an unchecked `rm` and always claimed NotOpened, so a failed removal leaked the owned directory unreported); never a guessed canonical value. (Canonicalizing at the CLOSE instead — the pre-fix bug — resolved a symlinked or moved scratch to the wrong tree, hid a surviving locked registration, and minted a false SnapshotClosed; reproduced with real git and mktemp.) A WET control proves the admin-dir handle on the REAL reader and close: `launcher_control_unreadable_worktrees_is_not_falsely_closed` runs the real scoped read with a post-checkout hook that LOCKS the registered worktree and chmods the main repo's `.git/worktrees` to 0300 (searchable and writeable but NOT readable), so `worktree list` exits 0 with an INCOMPLETE listing that OMITS the live registration and `rm -rf` has already cleared the checked-out tree — the close MUST report `CloseVerifyRegistrationPresenceUnobservable`, NEVER the false SnapshotClosed the pre-fix listing-only verifier minted (a hook marker written only when the lock AND chmod both succeeded proves the add actually registered before the sabotage, so it cannot pass for a never-registered add). Paths are passed as OPERANDS, never parsed as options: realpath is invoked `realpath -e -- <path>` and `shell.Remove.RecursiveForce` runs `rm -rf -- <path>` (a path beginning with '-' — a '-'-prefixed TMPDIR — used to be mis-parsed as a flag, so realpath failed AND the removal failed, leaking the scratch), and the realpath program is injectable on the scoped layout (`realpath_program`, like `git_program`) so the otherwise-unreachable pre-add arm is testable. The close runs EVERY removal (never an && chain that short-circuits), treats the removal exit codes as advisory only, and RE-ESTABLISHES cleanliness from observations: the runner's `worktree list --porcelain -z` (NUL records, so a path containing a NEWLINE is not split and missed, the way a newline-split once closed a live snapshot falsely) must no longer name the CANONICAL identity — and the registration verdict is bound to the ADD: the worktree's `.git` gitfile (`gitdir: <repo>/.git/worktrees/<id>`) is read WHILE the worktree exists, the ADMIN directory it names is CARRIED to the close, and `filesystem_entry_presence_of_path` on THAT admin dir (folding its parent listing) ESTABLISHES registration absence — because git can exit `worktree list` with code 0 and an INCOMPLETE listing when `.git/worktrees` is unreadable (mode 0300), silently omitting a live registration, which an earlier revision read as absence and minted a false SnapshotClosed while `rm -rf` had already cleared the checked-out tree. The `worktree list --porcelain -z` remains only as a SECOND, add-only check: one that names the carried identity is `CloseVerifyRegistrationPersists`, a NONZERO listing (a `.git` lock, or the very ownership refusal this family is about) is `CloseVerifyRegistrationPresenceUnobservable` carrying the exit and stderr, and even a clean 0-exit listing may only ADD present or unobservable — it NEVER establishes absence on its own, and a listing that never answered must not be reported as git saying the worktree is still there. AND `filesystem_entry_presence_of_path` on the canonical scratch AND on the original mktemp allocation (a distinct tree under a symlinked scratch) — which folds the PARENT listing, so an existing-but-UNREADABLE scratch reads as Present, not the false Absent the old Filesystem.List(...).success gave — must report ESTABLISHED absence for BOTH; only then does the close say Closed, and a present or unobservable half yields the typed SnapshotCloseVerificationFailed { verification } (CloseVerifyRegistrationPersists | CloseVerifyRegistrationPresenceUnobservable | CloseVerifyDirectoryPersists | CloseVerifyDirectoryPresenceUnobservable) which carries a CAUSE and never a fabricated exit_code:0. These close-verification semantics are covered hermetically by test.claim.roadmap.roadmap_event_carrier_witness (a_close_verification_failure_fails_toward_the_leak_and_names_its_cause, run by hand on the claim lane) — which checks that a SUPPLIED verification failure is correctly REPORTED (a failure standing, named, counted as a leak, never a fabricated exit code and never Closed); it does NOT exercise the verifier's filesystem/identity judgement, which the wet lane owns. No witness on either lane (hermetic or wet) is executed by CI, because CI runs none; both are run by hand, the hermetic ones on the mocked claim lane and the wet ones with real git. The production deploy job is the only place the launcher read is exercised for real, as the runner principal (ghrunner).",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
